WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Stealth ATF, something deadly in the air.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:27.680
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.540
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.500
And that's it again.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.960
Don't worry. Joe will be back.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.020
He promises. Joe will return in.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.880
I don't know. The next episode.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.440
Yeah. I'm pretty sure.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.400
I mean, we hope to see him in three stooges.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.760
That is the next episode.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.440
But maybe Joe used his stealth mode here and he's actually on the podcast.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.680
He's just invisible.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.060
Yeah. He's stealth AF.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.540
Yeah. Instead of ATF.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:05.000
For those of you that aren't aware of the game, stealth ATF has you controlling a Lockheed F-117.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:06.900
It doesn't sound right to me.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.500
That's too many numbers. Usually I'm used to like F-35.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.160
So F-117 must be just a bigger.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:25.840
It's the Lockheed one. The one that looks cool that you thought looked really cool in like the 90s when they started to like put out posters for, you know, warplanes.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.100
There you go.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.560
Yeah. It's time to start idolizing warplanes.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.460
The B-17 bomber is done.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.300
It's all about the Lockheed F-117 Nighthawk now.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:53.440
I will say that like, you know, this plane is probably like, you know, aside from just because like the F-15 or 35, whatever those actually are, are the most iconic just because of how common they are.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.400
Like this was like super cool.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.740
And I used to like think about this plane when I was a kid.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:14.380
Did you? I didn't think much about planes when I was a kid, but to be fair, you know, that's because, you know, just in general, I had a lack of interest in most things that didn't have to do with video game versions of them.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:20.780
So had I been exposed to stealth ATF, that might have ignited my love for the Lockheed F-117.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.760
Okay. I can grant that.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:46.840
Now, the stealth joke that I was making earlier about Joe comes because the whole like gimmick here and in the name of the title, stealth ATF, is that this plane has a stealth mode that can be used once per mission so that you can basically be invisible on enemy radars and they actually like lose sight of you and have no idea where you are.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.500
Sounds really cool.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.480
I don't know how well it works in the video game.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:51.460
I guess we'll get into that.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:03:03.720
But the overall idea here for this game is just that it's a first person, like you're in the cockpit, being briefed missions that are mostly just take down other enemy aircrafts.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.960
You know, the number just kind of increases or the types of enemies changes.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:22.840
But for the most part, that is your objective is to kind of go on these large battle, large empty battlefields, take down the enemy planes, successfully land your plane and then move on to the next mission.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.800
Sean, the game kind of feels like Top Gun here.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:40.580
Sure. I'd say that, you know, on the surface with, you know, the UI and the nature of, you know, what you're doing, like it bears some surface resemblances to Top Gun.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:42.780
But I think they're very different games.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:53.960
Whereas like Top Gun was kind of an arc, not quite an arcade experience, but it's still, I still think it played by those rules.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.880
This one is definitely trying to go more simulation.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.460
I don't know if you've got that vibe.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:18.040
Yeah, I wanted to talk about that because definitely just in the amount of information on your, on the user interface screen, whether you're in the cockpit or taking off or landing the plane, they are trying to go for something here where, you know, you're either in training or on an actual mission.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:31.140
The idea of taking off in the plane, then going out to the battlefield and landing the plane, like each one of those things is kind of its own element of plane simulation, right?

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:41.740
Like some, some games like Microsoft Flight Simulator 2020, which is like a big thing now, that game is mostly a landing simulator game.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:51.360
Like, yeah, you can play to like, you know, fly to find the, the airports or the landing places, but you could also just turn on GPS navigation for that stuff.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.920
The actual challenge is in the landing of it.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:55.000
That's a whole game.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:05:00.100
This game decides to make it like three different steps or three different modes, if you will.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:06.740
You know, the, the, the, the takeoff the runway, the shooting down of the enemies and the landing all into one concise thing.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.860
What did you think about the actual like control of the F-117?

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:27.100
Well, I know that Top Gun, um, and I don't want to just keep, just keep talking about this game in terms of Top Gun, but, uh, I mean, it's, it's very similar in this respect, where I know that there was like a takeoff and landing portion of that game too.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:38.100
Um, I'd say that in this version, uh, where they're trying to like show you that, you know, taking off and landing is hard, um, and requires skill.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:46.440
Uh, takeoff is just literally press a button and, and, you know, pull back on your D-pad and then you, you take off.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.500
Um, and that's cool, I guess, just to have that little bit of immersion in there.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:06:01.020
Uh, the actual flying around, uh, is its own thing that I'm sure we'll, we'll speak at length about, but landing itself.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:11.980
Um, I, it's, it's definitely hard, uh, at first before you get the hang of it and kind of understand the little tells that the plane has.

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:19.660
Um, uh, you're going to crash a lot and then once you get it, you're never going to crash.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:24.840
Uh, because it kind of is just a, a solved game, I guess.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:35.680
Um, but it is still cool that they have those three, uh, those three sort of, uh, aspects of flying in the game.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:40.900
I'm, I don't know, did you have anything that, that really bugged you about it?

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:49.780
Well, I guess I would say that you summed each part up really nice, especially that the taking off section of just like, yeah, I mean like what more do you really want to it?

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:59.540
But you really are just holding down the A button to rev up the engine and then just, you know, going down on the D-pad to, because it's inverted controls in order to go up.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:03.680
That is really easy, almost impossible to fail.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:23.060
Yeah. And I guess that from there though, like it goes from no challenge whatsoever, you know, thinking like, oh, maybe this will be like a fun arcade-y style game where like, you know, it's not, it's not like super, um, like I need to be aware of my altitude or I need to be aware of all these different things.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:37.540
That immediately is subverted when you get to the actual mission and suddenly, you know, you're greeted with a user interface that has your altitude, your fuel level, your heading, your angle of attack, how much damage is done to you.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.480
Like that's just a lot of information in general.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:44.680
Forget about the actual first person cockpit mode of it all.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:50.600
I just feel like, you know, did they need the whole like taking off thing?

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:58.700
Maybe they should have spent more time in game explaining what, you know, what was going on here in the actual cockpit.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.760
Now, granted, that's what a manual is for.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:07.180
I just feel like it's really hard to wrap your head around this mission thing when you're first get into the game.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:17.400
Yeah, it really is night and day in terms of simplicity and then complexity between takeoff and like the actual mission.

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:28.920
Again, it bears, it bears surface resemblance to Top Gun, but I did go back and play Top Gun and there really isn't nearly as much.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:35.040
Like freedom in Top Gun as there is in this game.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:42.860
And the I think the biggest thing is because like they're not trying to simulate a 3D environment.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:50.420
And this game does not actually simulate a 3D environment, but it's trying its damnedest to make you think it is.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:09:00.340
So what you get is a featureless ground or ocean or, you know, surface.

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:07.920
And then you get all of your bells and whistles on your screen, in your UI, in your cockpit.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:17.620
And then you've got, at first glance, just tiny little dots that are probably your enemies, both on the radar and out your windshield.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:30.340
And a lot of what makes this part kind of confusing is that, because I don't really think that they fully simulated a 3D environment.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:37.500
I think the part that makes this very difficult is that there's no visual indicator of how high you are.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:41.060
It's just the altitude number on your cockpit.

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:48.960
Otherwise, the game looks exactly the same when you're at 50,000 feet than it does at 10,000 feet.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:52.720
Like there's no like, oh wow, the trees look too big, I better pull up.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:54.520
It's just the number.

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.740
So that doesn't really help.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:11.080
So then there's this whole like, faux 3D space, which in some ways kind of works, but in some ways will just make your enemies sort of teleport.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:20.360
If they get off screen, and sometimes even when they're on your screen, they don't seem to behave like you obey the same laws of physics.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:34.360
It's almost like you're sort of, you're actually playing on a giant cylinder than like in just a space above like a city or a forest.

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:44.320
Like it's an odd, and I'm not really doing a good job describing it, but it just seems like the planet that you're on is shaped strangely.

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:58.300
Because like you'll turn around, and then something that was going in the same direction as you, and was never that far away from you, as soon as you like lose them, they're now like on the opposite side of the screen.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:05.340
I don't know if that's just like wraparound, because I just don't really know how space in this game works.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:22.580
Yeah, I think it is wraparound, and I think a lot of what you're describing here, Sean, is the problem that we had with a game a long time ago now called Star Voyager, which was a space simulation game where you could actually like find yourself going to other planets, and who knows what you would run into.

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:35.000
And it was more of a simulator game too, but the problem there was a game that was trying to be a simulation game while not understanding the limits of the NES itself.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:51.900
And this isn't the same problem as say what we had with like Nobunaga's ambition, where they figured out how to take all the complexities of strategy and divvy it out into like numbers that you would just look at the screen and basically be menu navigating the game.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:12:00.680
That might be a cool thing for like a game where you're like an air traffic controller, but not a game where you're in an F-117.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:14.600
So what they've done here is they focused so much on, you know, what I think is a very fateful adaptation of a really fast plane dealing with other really fast planes trying to be in a dogfight.

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:29.020
Like it's not easy to just shoot down another enemy plane, but because of the limitations of the NES itself and the fact that they couldn't like actually design any other elements than just ground and sky into it.

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:37.060
There really is no sense of space because there's just the horizontal line that angles itself with your angling.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:44.860
So at least you kind of get a sense of your upside down or sideways or something, but there's no, there's no sense of actual space.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:52.420
The wraparound thing becomes a problem because again, if you're going for realistic, that's not going to work here.

00:12:52.420 --> 00:13:09.500
But also the speed at which you run into the enemies means that this is going to be a much longer mission than what might appear to the player taking down for enemy aircrafts in any kind of shmup or most even like Top Gun, for instance.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:13.160
I think you said it earlier in the episode, John, that that was more of an arcade game.

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:17.820
I agree. I think Top Gun felt a little looser in the cockpit.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:22.940
You got to like select your different missiles before going into the missions.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:31.420
The plane sprites were like significantly larger and came at you from the sides, kind of like how a shmup works, how they appear on screen in this game.

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:43.500
Because everything is dealt more with like, honestly, if you guys have ever played like Star Fox 64, it's dealt like those levels where, you know, you enter the 3D space of those boss fights.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:51.060
It's it just doesn't work here because of the limited graphical capabilities of the NES, in my opinion.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:14:01.340
I think that the biggest problem that this game has, and again, it is just the graphical capabilities of it, is just how jerky it is.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:11.100
Like the biggest difference that I could find between Top Gun and this game in terms of playability is Top Gun is actually very smooth.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:23.080
Like the animation of your enemies of the stuff on the ground, like it, and just, you know, the particle effects, I'll put that in quotes, of like the clouds coming at you.

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:29.980
Like it really gives you a sense of where you are in this, in the sky here.

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:43.400
Whereas whenever you move, you're automatically, like just the littlest touch on the D-pad, that is like a 15 degree shift in like the horizon.

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:57.380
And then you've got these planes that, again, you're trying to simulate speed, but you're not going to have a different sprite for like 500 meters away and 490 meters away all the way down to right in front of you.

00:14:57.380 --> 00:15:03.620
So you just sort of got them shifting at maybe like 10 to 15 frames a second.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:10.180
It's just like this very fast flip book of a plane like flashing past you.

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:20.700
Now I will say though, that once you kind of get the hang of that, you could like, once you've trained your brain to think in 15 frames a second,

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:26.440
as one might have had to do if they had a really shitty computer back in the day.

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:30.560
But you can actually kind of have fun with these dogfights.

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:33.040
Like it's not, it's not impossible.

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:40.620
And I actually was able to kind of get the hang of it to an extent that like, it actually felt like I was flying a plane for a bit.

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:44.380
It's just, there's a lot of obstacles.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:53.320
Especially if you have never played a game like this on this kind of hardware that can really take you out of it.

00:15:54.040 --> 00:16:03.380
Yeah, you know, that's exactly what I was going to lead into next, Sean, is that this is just a thing with older hardware simulation games.

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:09.560
Is that at the time, right, they were kind of like top of the line simulators.

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:14.680
And so people just learned to, you know, they learned to get around them and understand the controls.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:17.980
And they spent probably more time with it than we were able to maybe in this podcast.

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:25.980
But even if you go back to older driving simulator games, like they don't actually simulate every aspect of driving.

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:30.280
Like they're more arcadey than we might expect them to be.

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:38.380
This game too, like obviously isn't the most realistic physics demonstration of an aircraft.

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:55.360
However, I think what this game was missing was at some point they needed to make a design decision over whether it was going to be a really intense dogfight between, you know, between you and the enemies.

00:16:55.360 --> 00:17:09.080
That and maybe like maybe make it more technical, slow everything down a little bit, giving you more time to focus on, you know, sneaking up on the enemy and being able to to hunt them down.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:12.100
Currently, I feel like it's just a little too fast for that.

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:22.080
Or they could have decided to do the opposite and maybe, you know, open the world up a little more in terms of not being able to just.

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:25.460
OK, everybody's here and now everybody's not here.

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:27.280
It should have maybe had more in it.

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:35.960
Yeah, I think that if they could have put some kind of landmark on the ground, and I know that that's not that's not entirely possible.

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:38.760
Like just to have something that you could that you can.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:45.760
Anchor yourself with aside from just a blank slate that would have helped.

00:17:45.880 --> 00:18:00.600
Another thing that would have helped is and this is actually this would fundamentally change the game and it they'd have to get creative and maybe get rid of their newfangled stealth mode that we haven't really touched upon.

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:05.580
But this game needed throttle up and throttle down.

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:14.800
Like you cannot have a dogfight really with with just like you're always going at the same speed like you can't slow down.

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:20.840
You can't speed up because both your face buttons are for either missiles or your machine gun.

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:27.880
And then you're up your D pad is for pitching yaw and all that.

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:32.480
So you need to have another button to go faster and slower.

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:46.720
That would I think bring a lot more to like being able to enjoy the dogfights in this game because otherwise the only way that I could do it is like, oh, I guess I got to do like a front flip or a back flip just to get everybody back in my field of view.

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:51.700
Otherwise, you weren't I just wasn't going to have a lot of fun with this game.

00:18:52.660 --> 00:19:01.820
Yeah. And it's one of those games where it becomes challenging to take down the enemy aircraft that actually becomes fun to when you when it happens.

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:08.500
You know, it actually feels exciting when you get a guy, whether that's through the machine gun or the lock on missiles.

00:19:08.500 --> 00:19:16.660
I think that the lock on missiles are very satisfying because you see the little square around the enemy and you know that you've been locked on.

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:18.300
It shoots the missile.

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:26.920
But then there's like a delay of a little bit because of the depth between you and the enemy aircraft that you're not quite sure if it landed.

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:32.860
And then you get that moment of success when it blows up and you realize that you've made a successful target.

00:19:33.040 --> 00:19:33.940
That's exciting.

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:45.060
That is exciting. And I think that the arcs that they take do look really cool because it still is because you have no idea how far away it is and maybe it's going to miss entirely.

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:48.180
But it is satisfying once you finally hit it.

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:55.680
One thing that I'd say is not very satisfying is just the amount of feedback that you get when you get hit.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:20:01.680
Because I have no idea aside from taking glances at my damage meter.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:05.500
Like I have no idea how I got hit or what hit me.

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:10.540
I think maybe there needed to be a little bit more feedback as to like when you take damage.

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:13.420
Maybe a little bit of red or like a screen shake.

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:20.660
I think right now just a it's just like a beep that sounds like different than other beeps.

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:30.860
Yeah and there's a lot of sound cues in the game so I think you know it was a conscious decision but yes they should have had some kind of flash as well or something.

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:35.580
I don't know if that would have been annoying depending on how much damage you actually take when the machine guns happen.

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:38.540
Like maybe that would have caused a seizure like effect.

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:49.620
But you know I would say that for the damage meter and all that stuff even just the way that like finding enemies and stuff like that.

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:59.460
Did it feel like the AI kind of was helping you discover them or be on the same like they were they were obviously hunting you too.

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:03.820
But like altitude never really mattered when it came to the dog fight.

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:09.860
Like if I was 50,000 feet they were 50,000 feet if I was 30,000 like they were there with me.

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:14.000
I didn't have to like calibrate myself to find them on an altitude level.

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:25.260
So for all I know like when they were even attacking me right it's like maybe they were attacking me from behind and there's just no real like way to know that.

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:31.820
Or maybe they were attacking me from an angled an angle of attack you know that was different than where I was altitude wise.

00:21:31.820 --> 00:21:35.280
But I'm not able to like discern that information from the cockpit.

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:37.220
You could argue that's part of the experience.

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:46.200
I think that you're right that the that the computer is helping you because again I really don't think that they've really modeled a 3D space here.

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:52.240
And more so have just have some clever tricks to make you think that you're in a 3D space.

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:57.400
Because you're right like they never seem to be all that far away.

00:21:57.820 --> 00:22:07.280
I think they're never always they're not always on the same like altitude as you but they're never like more than maybe 5,000 feet away from you.

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:14.180
Just so that you can have fights where you are like more angled towards the ground than not.

00:22:14.180 --> 00:22:17.740
So it doesn't feel like it's more of a two dimensional game.

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:24.440
But yeah they're never like you're never gonna lose them because you're way above them.

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:30.540
You're gonna more lose them because they're they're behind you and you need to flip around to see them.

00:22:41.500 --> 00:22:44.200
And the last part of combat here the stealth mode.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:52.260
Arguably the name of the game implies that this mode is is the cool feature which is that you hit the start button.

00:22:52.260 --> 00:23:00.400
And it allows you to go into a invisible cloaking for a small amount of time so that you can shake off the enemies.

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:06.760
I played the first level of this game without knowing that that was like a thing.

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:07.920
I didn't read the manual.

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:13.520
I didn't understand. I thought like the stealth thing was part of just the aircraft itself like part of the naming convention.

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:15.400
I don't know.

00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:21.880
It's a cool feature and everything but it doesn't actually like you know maybe I'm just so focused on trying to hit anything.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:28.040
Of the other enemy aircrafts that I'm not worried about necessarily myself and this damage feedback that I need to think about.

00:23:28.180 --> 00:23:29.640
Oh better turn on stealth mode.

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:30.780
You only get one use.

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:44.660
Yeah okay this this whole feature is a cop out and I think you're I don't I don't know for a fact but I think you're right in saying that the whole stealth idea for the aircraft is just the design of the aircraft.

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:54.900
Like it's got all these sharp edges and weird and weird designs because it was designed to like deflect radar and not reflect it.

00:23:55.020 --> 00:24:03.240
So the plane in real life is always in stealth mode and they just sort of made this up to make it a gameplay feature.

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:11.500
And I think it's kind of a dumb gameplay feature that you press start once and then all missiles just don't lock on anymore.

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:16.580
It's not fun like there's no like cool ways to play around with it.

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:18.380
It's just a get out of jail free card.

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:25.180
I think we've exhausted talking about this first person cockpit experience as much as we possibly can.

00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:28.600
And I think anything else really needs to be experienced by the listener.

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:37.640
So I would say if this game has intrigued you at all just from a complexity standpoint you're worth trying the game here because maybe you'll find a different experience.

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:49.100
I personally just feel like the the combat mission portion is just lacking important information and then giving you a lot of additional information that maybe isn't necessarily required.

00:24:49.700 --> 00:25:02.880
Yes. So then we move on to landing.

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:14.100
Sean you tease this a little bit at the beginning because I think I think you put it very nicely that this is a solved game but a very hard one to to solve.

00:25:14.420 --> 00:25:17.240
Once you figure it out it's the same thing every time.

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:22.880
However figuring it out is left to a trial and error that results in a lot of crashes.

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:28.940
And crashes don't mean that you just redo the landing you redo the whole mission.

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:34.060
Yeah. It's pretty rough there and it's very frustrating.

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:37.140
But yeah.

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:42.600
So then this mode like your face buttons are no longer shoot and missile.

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:45.460
They're now throttle up and throttle down.

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:56.320
And basically the trick here is you want to sort of you want to kind of lean into the landing but also throttle down.

00:25:56.640 --> 00:26:07.260
And then you want to as you're getting slower and getting closer to the runway you want to sort of pull the nose up and continue to slow down.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:10.760
But you don't want to go so slow that you stall and fall.

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:26.060
And then at some certain point you won't realize that you touched the ground because it does a very bad job of telling you where the ground is because it's like in this faux landscape where you're not landing on a flat line.

00:26:26.060 --> 00:26:30.220
You're landing in like a perspective where there's a horizon behind you.

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:32.340
So you're not really sure where the ground is.

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:37.600
But then you'll just realize that you've been at that same level and haven't crashed.

00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:39.920
So there you know that you've landed.

00:26:40.580 --> 00:26:44.000
Wow. Pretty impressive stuff, Sean, to walk us through like that.

00:26:44.740 --> 00:26:54.520
I think what's even funnier, though, is like, you know, that is a very detailed explanation of what you can do to figure out how to land this plane properly.

00:26:54.880 --> 00:27:03.720
The 2022 solution is to just look up a picture or a video of a proper landing in this game and just mirror the exact stats.

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:09.860
Because as long as the lines and the pictures like I'm not talking about the actual aircraft landing in a specific spot.

00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:11.900
No, I'm talking about in the user interface.

00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:19.280
There's all these different things that they put up on the on the landing screen to kind of show you like first.

00:27:20.020 --> 00:27:21.880
Yeah, I know. It's it's the same thing with Top Gun.

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:25.960
Remember where we were complaining about the landing there and you revealed that it tells you.

00:27:26.200 --> 00:27:27.840
But now Top Gun is more forgiving.

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:30.640
That actually gives you the exact numbers you need to hit.

00:27:30.640 --> 00:27:43.780
This would be pretty interesting if it actually made it so that you needed to like trace the lines over like it had the actual angles that you want to be at and you just kind of have to line yourself up with them.

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:47.140
I'd argue then what's the point of even simulating it then at that point.

00:27:47.360 --> 00:27:57.080
Yeah. But what we get here is arguably a more frustrating experience, if only because they chose not to just restart from the landing mission.

00:27:57.080 --> 00:28:00.100
Landing is tricky and they knew it was tricky.

00:28:00.320 --> 00:28:01.640
Trust me. I mean, come on.

00:28:02.120 --> 00:28:03.280
And here we go.

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:06.840
Starting way back to the beginning is just cruel.

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:19.080
Thankfully, I played this on an emulator because this would have this would have taken me out of the game immediately to know that like all my hard work in the dogfight immediately is undone.

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:24.320
This just doesn't happen in modern video games at all, but it shouldn't be happening back then either.

00:28:24.500 --> 00:28:34.620
There are they are able to like create states to be like, OK, you're now in the like if you're going to even name these things right like one one is the first takeoff.

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:36.100
One two is the mission.

00:28:36.580 --> 00:28:38.180
One three is the landing.

00:28:38.360 --> 00:28:42.600
You should be able to just start from the beginning of one three every time you crash in the landing.

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:47.920
Yeah, it's it's kind of a tough thing to balance there.

00:28:48.080 --> 00:29:01.080
I almost feel like they shouldn't even punish you at all and maybe just give you like a points bonus or something or like access to a bonus like mini game after if you pull it off on the first try.

00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:09.640
Because punishing you at the end of a completely different game with this with this little test of memorization.

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:12.680
It's it's beyond me.

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:20.320
I think that they needed to just let you land without any punishment and just reward you if you did it correctly.

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:24.480
Right. We need to talk to about the length of time.

00:29:24.700 --> 00:29:30.080
You know, the takeoff takes all of 10 to 15 seconds to get up in the air.

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:37.480
Where the landing sequence could take maybe as much as 30 seconds because they give you a long runway in most of the levels.

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:41.000
I think there is one where you have to land on a carrier, which is just crazy.

00:29:41.320 --> 00:29:45.860
But, you know, for the most part, they're just very infinitely long landing strips.

00:29:46.700 --> 00:29:49.320
You know, that probably takes about 30 seconds.

00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:53.420
The dogfight isn't like some one to two minute experience.

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:54.820
That's going to take some time.

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:57.080
Yeah, it's like six to seven minutes at least.

00:29:57.380 --> 00:29:58.520
Right to hunt everybody down.

00:29:58.520 --> 00:30:05.520
So I don't know, like being forced to, you know, to do the whole like seven minute sequence again.

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:06.800
That's a little wild.

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:10.340
Yeah, that's that was poorly thought out.

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:17.920
However, you know, I could understand where they're coming from, too, of like crashing is failing the mission and the mission started at the base.

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:19.800
It's a different game, though.

00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:22.340
Like it's a completely different mode.

00:30:22.780 --> 00:30:28.000
And there should be, like you said, checkpoints between shifts in game.

00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:37.620
Like, yeah, of course, maybe you shouldn't get a checkpoint after you kill each enemy plane because like that's that's part of the game.

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:40.820
You have to you have to fight all of these and not them one at a time.

00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:45.900
But then you've just completely changed the perspective, the controls and your objective.

00:30:46.180 --> 00:30:48.480
So it makes more sense.

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:50.440
Maybe give them a little leeway.

00:30:51.140 --> 00:30:58.020
It's like crashing your car in the career mode of Gran Turismo and that being considered a player death and you have to start a new file.

00:30:58.660 --> 00:30:59.540
Gran Turismo.

00:30:59.860 --> 00:31:01.700
Yeah, you get five lives to start.

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:04.080
Yeah. Funny enough.

00:31:04.180 --> 00:31:06.960
I don't think they've ever let you crash cars in Gran Turismo.

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:09.540
Right. The car manufacturers probably would not allow that.

00:31:09.540 --> 00:31:13.000
I think the most you'll see is like broken windows.

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:14.380
I don't even know if they do that.

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:18.640
Mm hmm. Well, here, too, with the F-117, it's all or nothing.

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:25.120
You know, damage doesn't appear to do anything when it's just, you know, anywhere under 100 percent damage.

00:31:25.120 --> 00:31:27.280
It looks like the same great looking airplane.

00:31:27.640 --> 00:31:34.320
However, you know, 100 percent damage or a crash in the landing sequence and you're up in oblivion.

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:35.780
You have been evaporated.

00:31:43.420 --> 00:31:46.460
Now, how about the variety of these missions?

00:31:46.640 --> 00:31:51.320
Right. And I'm considering a mission, the whole thing, the takeoff, the dogfight and the landing.

00:31:51.840 --> 00:32:01.360
Is there anything more here than just like the amount of enemies you take on or then, you know, the the setting?

00:32:01.640 --> 00:32:03.140
They try to change it this way.

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:04.100
It's like now it's at night.

00:32:04.280 --> 00:32:05.360
Now it's over water.

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:07.920
Like, what do you think about the actual variety?

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:19.880
The only thing that I think was cool and it wasn't just like, well, I would hope that they'd have like different colors for things like and it's not all just above the same grassland.

00:32:21.020 --> 00:32:29.740
The one thing that I did think was cool was was the night missions because they do kind of have a different vibe about them.

00:32:29.920 --> 00:32:34.580
And like your your your UI looks different.

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:43.440
The planes have a different look against the sky and the takeoff and landing look very different as well.

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:52.840
So I even though it is mostly just cosmetic, having the night missions, I think, was a plus that they did feel cool.

00:32:55.620 --> 00:33:08.600
On the sequels and spinoff side here, this game was published by Activision, but the developer is Imagineering, which is not to be confused with Walt Disney's Imagineering.

